    "evidence_text": "Related Works\tThe focus of this section is on recently published datasets and LID research applicable to the South African context.\n\nIn South Africa, a multilingual corpus of academic texts produced by university students with different mother tongues is being developed .\n\nLanguage Identification (NLI) (determining the native language of the author of a text) and Language Variety Identification (LVI) (classification of different varieties of a single language) which opens up more datasets.\n\nThe NCHLT text corpora  is likely a good starting point for a shared LID task dataset for the South African languages .\n\nResearchers have recently started applying existing algorithms for tasks like neural machine translation in earnest to such South African language datasets .\n\nExisting NLP datasets, models and services  are available for South African languages.\n\nbased classifier is then used to predict the specific language within a language group.\n\nFor the South African languages this is done for the Nguni and Sotho groups.\n\nThe accuracies reported are for classifying a piece of text by its specific language label.\n\nConclusion\tLID of short texts, informal styles and similar languages remains a difficult problem which is actively being researched.\n\nFurther research opportunities include data harvesting, building standardised datasets and shared tasks for South Africa as well as the rest of Africa.",
